        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-4x+1?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-4x+1$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-6$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-6$ is perpendicular to the given line."
