        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-72528",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-5x-2?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-5x-2$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-5.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{5}x-8$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{5}x-8$ is perpendicular to the given line."
